#Engineering Architecture and Functional Reference

Purpose of this document: describe what the service is, what each part does, and
the process run against it, in enough detail for another engineer to take over.
Every statement names an identifier that can be checked against the code. Where a
name is a real term from an established field, it is labeled as such. Facts that
could read as problems are stated without evaluation.

Verified against the repository and GitHub Actions run history on 2026-07-28.

---

## 1. What the service does

A deterministic HTTP service that converts Washington State legislative bill text
into plain-English "plain-meaning" output. No AI model is called in the parsing or
rendering path; every transformation is rule-based JavaScript. Input is Washington
bill text (fetched by bill number, or supplied directly). Output is structured
plain-language text stating, per sentence, who must / may / cannot do what, and
under what conditions.

## 2. Runtime and how to run it

- Language: JavaScript, ES modules (`package.json` `"type": "module"`).
- Node.js: version 22 or higher (`engines.node: ">=22"`).
- Runtime dependencies: `express` ^4.18 (HTTP server), `@upstash/redis` ^1.34.
- Start: `node server.js` (`scripts.start`). Listens on `PORT` (3000 in local and
  workflow runs; the health check hits `http://localhost:3000/api/health`).
- Build the bill index: `node scripts/populate-bill-index.js` (`scripts.build`) —
  writes `data/wa/bill-index.json`.
- Entry point `server.js`: JSON body limit 2 MB; serves `/lib` as static files
  (so browser ES modules resolve); serves top-level HTML pages; registers an
  11-route API table; listens on `PORT`.

## 3. End-to-end request path (what happens to a bill)

1. Client requests a bill by number → `GET /api/wa-bill-text` → `api/wa-bill-text.js`
   fetches document text from `app.leg.wa.gov` (`.../bi/tld/documentsearchresults`),
   resolving the biennium (the two-year legislative period) from the year.
2. `lib/wa-adapter/index.js` `getNormalizedBill(biennium, billNumber)` normalizes the
   fetched bill into the shape the pipeline consumes.
3. Client POSTs bill text to `POST /api/plain-meaning` → `api/plain-meaning.js`:
   - `{ text }` body → `runPipeline(text)` (`lib/plain-meaning/pipeline.js`) →
     ISC output → `renderISC()` (`lib/plain-meaning/renderer.js`) → plain-meaning text.
   - `{ units }` body → skips the pipeline, goes straight to `renderISC()`
     (see §6 note on this path).
4. The response returns the plain-meaning text plus per-sentence records
   (`sentences[]`, `lineage.records`) that the test checks read.

External data sources the code contacts: `app.leg.wa.gov` (bill text) and
`wslwebservices.leg.wa.gov/legislationservice.asmx` (health-check reachability ping).

## 4. The parsing pipeline (`lib/plain-meaning/pipeline.js`)

Exported entry point: `runPipeline(rawText, context = {})`. About 1,580 lines. The
file header declares it deterministic, no AI, each layer a pure function. Ten layers,
run in order (acronyms are project-internal names defined in the file header, not
standard external terminology):

- L1  5WIH — who/what/when/where/why/how assembly
- L2  SSE  — source statement extraction (signal-anchored spans)
- L3  CFS  — constraint filter (removes intent, emotion, narrative)
- L4  LNS  — language normalization
- L5  AAC  — actor-action-condition parsing
- L6  TPS  — temporal parsing (deadlines, triggers, sequence)
- L7  SJM  — system/jurisdiction mapping
- L8  MPS  — mechanism parsing (how + enforcement)
- L9  RDS  — risk decomposition (likelihood vs consequence)
- L10 ISC  — information set construction (assembles the object the renderer consumes)

Central design assumption to hold onto: the pipeline derives exactly one actor, one
action, and one modal (must / may / cannot) per sentence. Real legislative sentences
that violate this one-to-one assumption are the recurring source of parsing defects.
The known violation patterns are handled by targeted code, including:

- `detectSecondInstruction` / `parseActorActionCondition` (L5) — detect a second
  actor+modal joined by "and", so a coordinate clause is not collapsed into one
  action string. `parseActorActionCondition` returns one or more
  `{actor, modal, action, conditions}` groups.
- Relative-clause modal handling (which / that / who) — bounds a relative clause so
  its own modal cannot override the main clause's modal.
- including / excluding list detection — marks a list span as absent during
  actor/action parsing so list items are not mis-read as the action.

Key functions: `runPipeline` (entry), `splitSentences`, `detectSecondInstruction`,
`parseActorActionCondition`, `signalForModal`, and the inclusion/exclusion list
detectors.

Meaning lineage (provenance): every transformation records what it did, so output can
be traced back to source text. Records live on the pipeline output
(`lineage.records`, each with `position` `[start, end]`, `text`, `producedBy`, `id`)
and drive two of the test checks (C4, L1). The renderer-side tracer is
`traceRenderUnit`. Project plain-language name for this: "meaning lineage" — a chain
of custody for meaning.

## 5. The renderer (`lib/plain-meaning/renderer.js`)

Exports:
- `renderISC(iscOutput, { debug })` — top level; turns the pipeline's ISC output into
  plain-meaning text. Prepends a section-type prefix to each section's first sentence,
  one of: `New law — `, `Amends existing law — `, `Funding — `, `Effective <date> — `.
- `renderUnit(unit, { debug })` — renders a single unit.
- `traceRenderUnit(unit)` — provenance tracer.

`analyzeCoordinateSplit` handles the same-actor "and"-joined coordinate case on the
renderer side (distinct from L5's different-actor case). Sentence templates come from
`templates.js` (`TEMPLATES`, `cleanActor`, `cleanAction`).

## 6. HTTP API (route table declared in `server.js`)

Eleven routes:

- `GET  /api/health` — bill index load status + WA Legislature reachability.
- `GET  /api/testing-stats` — corpus size + cumulative tested-bill count (for the UI
  validation disclaimer). Reads `bill-index.json` and `test-results.json`.
- `GET  /api/wa-bill-search` — keyword search over the bill index.
- `GET  /api/wa-bill-detail` — metadata and status for a bill number.
- `GET  /api/wa-bill-documents` — documents available for a bill.
- `GET  /api/wa-bill-text` — bill text by number (fetches `leg.wa.gov`).
- `GET  /api/wa-bill-selection` — classifies bill sentences (obligation / permission /
  condition / …) and extracts references.
- `GET  /api/wa-bill-plain-summary` — returns HTTP 410 (discontinued; replaced by
  `/api/plain-meaning`).
- `POST /api/analyze` — returns HTTP 410 (discontinued; replaced by
  `/api/plain-meaning`).
- `POST /api/plain-meaning` — main endpoint. `{ text }` runs `runPipeline` then
  `renderISC`; `{ units }` skips to `renderISC`. `MAX_TEXT_LENGTH` 50,000 chars.
  CORS open. `Cache-Control: no-store`.
- `GET  /api/openapi` — full OpenAPI 3.1 spec for the API (OpenAPI is the field-standard
  contract format for an HTTP API).

Fact to know, stated without evaluation: the `{ units }` path and the OpenAPI text
describe pre-processed "ISC units from the TCS Python pipeline" as the primary,
higher-fidelity path and `{ text }` / `runPipeline` as fallback. `CLAUDE.md` records
that this TCS integration is confirmed inactive and that no external pipeline currently
produces or consumes that schema. In the running service, the `{ text }` → `runPipeline`
path is therefore the active one. The `{ units }` schema is retained as an integration
contract per `CLAUDE.md`.

## 7. Data files (`data/wa/`)

- `bill-index.json` — the searchable bill index (list of bills with `bill_number` and
  related fields); rebuilt by `populate-bill-index.js`.
- `test-results.json` — persisted state of the system harness. Keys: `latestResults`
  (one entry per bill, keyed by bill number, overwritten in place), `coverageCursor`
  (integer; where the next run resumes in the bill pool), `coveragePasses` (count of
  completed full passes), `coverageSeenBillNumbers` (list),
  `cumulativeStats { testedBills, testedBillNumbers[], byCheck { C1..L1: {passed, failed} } }`.
- `test-results-archive.json` — archived prior results.
- `test-bills.json` — system-harness config, including the fixed sentinel bills run
  every run.
- `modal-test-cases.json`, `list-boundary-test-cases.json`, `and-split-test-cases.json`,
  `split-instruction-test-cases.json` — expected-answer fixtures for the four rule
  harnesses.

## 8. Testing

Two kinds of tests. They check different things; do not conflate them.

### 8a. System harness — `scripts/test-bills.js`

Runs five output checks against a running local server, over real bill text. Field
term: a **test harness** (code that drives inputs through the system and checks its
outputs). The five checks are **invariant / property checks** — they assert properties
the output must always hold. They are NOT comparisons against hand-authored correct
summaries. Exact definitions as implemented:

- **C1** (`scoreC1`) — output is non-empty and contains no `"Failure to comply:"`
  string. (well-formedness / smoke check)
- **C4** (`scoreC4`) — for each rendered sentence carrying an `anchorText` (skipping
  fallback-lens sentences), the whitespace-normalized anchor must appear as a substring
  of the source section text. On failure it runs a lineage-divergence diagnosis. Field
  term: a **faithfulness / groundedness check** — output must trace to source, not be
  invented.
- **C5** (`scoreC5`) — output must not contain a dollar-spacing corruption matching
  `/$\d+,\s\d{3}/` (e.g. `"$1, 000"`). (formatting-artifact check)
- **C6** (`scoreC6`) — output must not contain known corruption artifacts (`"bE"`; a
  stray capitalized Cyrillic `"З"`/`"С"` preposition; `"Failure to comply:"`) and must
  not contain duplicate paragraphs (provenance-aware deduplication using each
  paragraph's `anchorText`). (corruption + duplication check)
- **L1** (`scoreL1`) — position fidelity: for each lineage record with a `position`
  `[start, end]`, slicing the source section text at those offsets must be byte-exactly
  equal to the record's stored `text`. Field term: a **provenance / position-integrity
  check**.

Harness mechanics:

- It walks the whole bill pool in fixed numeric order using `coverageCursor`, so
  coverage accumulates across runs rather than sampling at random (systematic sampling
  with a persisted cursor). `latestResults` is an index keyed by bill number
  (overwrite-in-place), not an append log.
- The `sentinel` bills in `test-bills.json` run every run — known-tricky inputs kept as
  a standing guard (field term: **canary cases**).
- A known, documented failure is converted from fail to expected — field term **XFAIL
  (expected failure)** — so it does not count as a new break.
- Network/server failures are separated from real defects (`classifyFetchFailure`:
  "unreachable" vs "error"), so a WA Legislature outage is not scored as a pipeline
  break (guards against **flaky tests**).
- Accuracy boundary, stated plainly: these checks certify the output is well-formed and
  traceable to source. They do NOT certify that the plain-English meaning is correct.

### 8b. Rule harnesses — `scripts/test-modal-classifier.js`, `test-list-boundary.js`, `test-and-split-rule.js`, `test-split-instructions.js`

Each is an **example-based (golden) regression suite**: a fixed set of real
bill-derived sentences paired with confirmed-correct expected answers, held in the
matching `*-test-cases.json`. Each targets one detector. They call the exported
`runPipeline` (testing through the public interface, not private functions). This is
where meaning-correctness is actually checked, on curated cases.

## 9. Workflows (`.github/workflows/`) — with verified firing state

Four operational workflows. Firing state below verified from Actions run history on
2026-07-28.

- **`populate-bill-index.yml`** — trigger: daily cron `'0 6 * * *'` (06:00 UTC) plus
  manual (`workflow_dispatch`). Runs the index script, then commits the updated
  `bill-index.json` back to the repo (a self-committing / write-back workflow).
  Verified: active; firing daily; success.
- **`build-bill-corpus.yml`** — trigger: daily cron `'30 6 * * *'` (06:30 UTC) plus
  manual. Rebuilds the full-text corpus and uploads it as a build artifact with
  `retention-days: 90`. Does NOT commit it (comment: too large to commit). Field term:
  **build artifact** (produced by a run, stored by CI, not in the repo). The workflow
  holds `contents: write` permission but writes nothing back — an unused/over-broad
  grant. Verified: active; firing daily; success.
- **`run-tests.yml`** — trigger: manual only (`workflow_dispatch`), input `sample_size`
  (default `'30'`, passed as `TEST_SAMPLE_SIZE`). Steps: start server; poll
  `/api/health` until ready (readiness gate); download the previous `test-results`
  artifact from the last 20 successful runs to resume the coverage cursor
  (**artifact-passed state**); download the latest `bill-corpus` artifact from the last
  10 successful corpus runs into `data/wa/bill-corpus.json` so the run reads the corpus
  instead of live government servers (**hermetic testing**); run
  `scripts/test-bills.js`; then commit `test-results.json` to the repo AND upload it as
  an artifact, both with `if: always()` (results persist even when checks fail).
  Verified: last run 2026-07-19, success.
- **`pipeline-pr-check.yml`** — trigger: `pull_request` touching paths
  `lib/plain-meaning/**`, `scripts/test-bills.js`, or `data/wa/test-bills.json` (a path
  filter). Steps: start server; readiness poll; snapshot current `test-results.json` as
  a baseline (`cp` → `/tmp`); run the harness at `TEST_SAMPLE_SIZE '40'`; compare
  current vs baseline. Gate rule: a failing check blocks the merge only if that same
  bill+check passed before or was never sampled before; a check already failing in the
  baseline is reported but does not block (a **regression-only failure policy**). Field
  term: a **required status check** acting as a **regression gate**. Verified: firing on
  PRs; most recent run conclusion `action_required` (paused pending required approval).

Temporary workflows (`tmp-*.yml`, plus older debug ones) also exist. Project
convention: a single-use diagnostic/data-gathering workflow is placed on the default
branch only because GitHub's `workflow_dispatch` trigger recognizes a workflow only on
the default branch, and is meant to be deleted after the data is collected. Fact,
stated without evaluation: several `tmp-*` scripts, workflows, and their large
results `.txt` files are currently present on `main`.

Registry-vs-branch caveat: `gh workflow list --all` returns 14 workflows, all
`active`; the default branch holds 7 workflow files. In GitHub Actions, `active` means
a workflow is neither manually disabled nor auto-disabled for inactivity — it does NOT
mean a file for it exists on the branch. Deleting a workflow file leaves its registry
entry and run history in place. Firing state must therefore be read from run history,
not from the workflow list.

## 10. The working loop (the process run repeatedly)

1. Run the harness: trigger `run-tests` (manual). It runs the five checks over a sample
   of real bills, resuming coverage from `coverageCursor`.
2. Results land in `data/wa/test-results.json` (committed to the repo) and as an
   artifact. `cumulativeStats.byCheck` gives pass/fail per check; `latestResults` gives
   per-bill detail.
3. Analyze: when a check fails on real bill text, identify the specific bill and the
   grammatical pattern that caused it. Project rule: no fix is made without a concrete
   real-bill example proving the problem occurs.
4. Fix on a branch (never push to `main`). Open a pull request.
5. `pipeline-pr-check` gates the PR: it re-runs the harness on a 40-bill sample and
   blocks only on new regressions (a check that used to pass and now fails), not on
   pre-existing failures.
6. Merge; the branch auto-deletes (repo setting).
7. When a fix targets a specific grammatical pattern, its case is added to the matching
   rule harness (§8b) so the fix is locked as a golden case and cannot silently
   regress.
8. Independently, the two scheduled workflows keep `bill-index.json` (committed daily)
   and the corpus artifact (daily) current.

Branch/PR discipline (rules to follow): changes go on a branch → PR → merge; never push
directly to `main`. Exception: a single-use diagnostic workflow may be pushed to `main`
only because `workflow_dispatch` requires it, and is deleted right after.

